Description: Show the screen each tab had when the session ended, dimmed, when it is restored.
 After a crash, a reset of the machine or the end of the desktop session, Ptyxis restores its
 tabs in the directories they had, with new shells, which show nothing of what the tab was used
 for. The session now also saves the text of the screen of each tab, its last rows without
 colors, and a restored tab shows it in the dim style above the prompt of its new shell, so
 that it is clear what each terminal was doing, as Terminal on macOS does.
 .
 The screens are kept in $XDG_STATE_HOME/org.gnome.Ptyxis/screens.gvariant, readable only by
 the user, apart from the session state in the config directory, and linked to its tabs by an
 ID. They are saved with the session, which saves 3 seconds after a change, now also of a
 terminal's screen. Closing the last window saves its tabs without the screens and removes the
 screens file, so the screens are shown only after a session which ended with windows open. A
 terminal scrolled back shows earlier rows, so its screen is kept as it was the last time it
 was scrolled to the end.
 .
 The dim style is SGR 2, which VTE draws with the foreground color scaled to 2/3. That greys out
 light text on a dark background but darkens dark text on a light one.
